# Vulnerability details

## Impact
An integer overflow can lead to unexpected behavior in a smart contract, potentially causing financial loss or disruption of the contract's intended functionality.

## Proof of Concept
If the input value passed to the function exceeds 64 bits, an integer overflow vulnerability can arise in the code. This is due to the line of code where the input value, which is 32 bytes, is first converted to a 256-bit integer and then back to a 64-bit unsigned integer using n = uint64(uint256(lenum >> 192)). Such a process can result in truncation of the input value and cause an integer overflow. A hacker can take advantage of this vulnerability by submitting a large input value that causes an integer overflow. This overflow can cause unexpected behavior of the contract, such as incorrect calculations, unexpected results, or even a complete system failure.

	pragma solidity =0.8.12;
	import "./Endian.sol";

	contract Attacker {
    	       function attack(bytes32 lenum) public {
       	 	uint64 n = Endian.fromLittleEndianUint64(lenum);
        		uint64 largeValue = uint64(2 ** 65);
       		 uint64 result = n * largeValue; 

   	     }
	}

A hacker can exploit the code shown above by invoking the attack function with a sizeable input value. The multiplication operation with the n value will then result in an integer overflow. The product obtained from this operation can be utilized for carrying out malicious activities, including fund theft or altering the state of the contract.


## Tools Used
VSCODE
## Recommended Mitigation Steps
To mitigate this vulnerability, the Endian library should be updated to handle input values larger than 64 bits or to restrict the input size to ensure that it does not exceed 64 bits. 



## Assessed type

Under/Overflow

